    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.jump\n\n### Description\n\nFeature request: Provide an option for `fFtT` to follow the direction of the initial jump in vim-like manner.\n\nCurrently, `f` means forward and `F` means backward from the perspective of the cursor. However, IMO it makes more sense that e.g. `f` means forward *from the direction of the initial search*. This is more consistent with vim's behavior of e.g. `n` and `N` where they go forward and backward in the direction of whether `?` or `/` was used to search backward/forward respectively. I would even argue this should be a better default. \n\nThis is especially important if one wants to remap both `,` and `;` to repeat (since they can just rely on `fFtT` to repeat)--then they can only depend on `fFtT` to repeat in a way they find intuitive. I believe the suggested behavior is also the default behavior of some plugins mini.jump might be inspired by.\n\n---------\n\nI first noticed the discrepancy in behavior when trying similar plugins and thought mini.jump default behavior seems simpler but then realized I relied on `;` for repeats only in mini.jump because the direction of the repeats with `fFtT` did not feel as natural for the reason mentioned.",
